1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:15,000
The video I'm about to show is the first time that I'm seeing any of the hunger strikers mentioned on mainstream news in any kind of detail, even if those details are wrong and skewed. Allow me to help.

2
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,920
A Palestine action activist who's been on a 46-day hunger strike in prison has been taken to hospital after a condition deteriorated.

3
00:00:22,920 --> 00:00:40,800
Well, that makes it sound like the oh-so-benevolent prison staff at HMP Brunsfield just called the ambulance, but that's not true. Activists and even MP Zahra Sultana had to protest outside to get the prison to finally call and allow for an ambulance to take Kesa to hospital.

4
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:48,180
Kesa Zahra was in HMP Brunsfield awaiting trial over an alleged break-in as an Israel-based defence firm.

5
00:00:48,180 --> 00:00:54,200
An alleged break-in, not an act of terrorism, yet counter-terrorism powers have been used against them.

6
00:00:54,520 --> 00:01:03,820
And it's not a defence firm, it's a weapons manufacturer called Elbert Systems, and it operates on British soil making drones that are used to kill Palestinians.

7
00:01:04,540 --> 00:01:06,620
Jane Deeth joins me now with more on this. Jane.

8
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:15,260
Yes, Kesa Zahra has been refusing food for almost two months now. She's waiting to be tried over the alleged break-in at the defence company.

9
00:01:15,960 --> 00:01:17,140
Weapons manufacturer.

10
00:01:17,140 --> 00:01:20,140
She joins five other prisoners refusing food.

11
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:24,480
There are actually seven other hunger strikers. Kesa is one of eight.

12
00:01:24,740 --> 00:01:27,580
Some charged also in connection with the defence company.

13
00:01:28,480 --> 00:01:30,180
Weapons manufacturer.

14
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:34,020
Others accused of breaking into RAF Bryce Norton.

15
00:01:34,740 --> 00:01:45,820
Their collective refusal of food is believed to be the biggest hunger strike since 1981, the IRA hunger strike in the Mays prison, where 23 prisoners refused food,

16
00:01:45,820 --> 00:01:48,580
and 10 of them starved themselves to death.

17
00:01:48,580 --> 00:01:59,260
Starved themselves to death removes any blame from the British government for not engaging with a very reasonable demand of wanting to be recognised as political prisoners,

18
00:01:59,260 --> 00:02:03,580
which they were, and the Filton 24 clearly are.

19
00:02:03,580 --> 00:02:13,320
The lawyers for these activists say that they were charged before Palestine action was banned by this government under terrorism legislation.

20
00:02:13,480 --> 00:02:19,060
But nonetheless, they claim that since the group was prescribed, they faced harsher treatment in prison,

21
00:02:19,460 --> 00:02:25,860
saying they'll all face over a year in prison before they get to any trial and that their communications have been restricted.

22
00:02:25,860 --> 00:02:29,860
People can be held on remand for a maximum of six months.

23
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,300
There is a possibility to extend it if it is absolutely necessary.

24
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:39,820
But for an alleged break-in, even six months would be extreme.

25
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:43,800
What they're demanding is that they are all immediately released on bail.

26
00:02:44,320 --> 00:02:52,380
This afternoon, dramatic events, Kesa Zura did leave HMP Bronzefield, but in an ambulance, critically ill, her supporters say.

27
00:02:52,380 --> 00:02:57,300
I spoke to a friend of hers, Ella, who said that she spoke to Kesa yesterday evening,

28
00:02:57,440 --> 00:03:04,260
who reported being so weak she was unable to stand, and claims that she was asking for that ambulance yesterday.

29
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:12,220
Asking for an ambulance for an entire day, but it only arrived once activists took action outside of the prison.

30
00:03:12,600 --> 00:03:18,780
Had they not done that, it is very possible that the prison would have left Kesa to die in her cell.

31
00:03:19,420 --> 00:03:20,360
What's the government saying?

32
00:03:20,360 --> 00:03:29,600
Well, I spoke to the Ministry of Justice this evening who told me that prisoners who refuse food are continually assessed by prison healthcare staff

33
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:35,340
who do take appropriate action, including arranging hospital treatment when it's required.

34
00:03:35,880 --> 00:03:37,000
Clearly that didn't happen.

35
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:38,720
So far, David Lammy...

36
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:40,060
Absolute waste, man.

37
00:03:40,220 --> 00:03:45,780
...has not granted the request to meet the lawyers and MPs of the six people on hunger strike,

38
00:03:45,900 --> 00:03:49,800
but the government says he is being kept informed of the situation.

39
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:50,980
Oh, fantastic.

40
00:03:51,200 --> 00:03:52,380
So no need for us to worry.

41
00:03:52,920 --> 00:03:58,560
Follow Prisoners for Palestine to stay updated and share and amplify the voices of the hunger strikers

42
